What I Learned
This week covered open source licensing, how the shell interprets what I type, and how to find documentation for commands I don't know.
Source code must be compiled into machine instructions before a computer can run it. Closed source licenses let users run a program but never see or modify its code, while open source licenses give users the right to view, change and redistribute it, as defined by bodies like the Free Software Foundation and the Open Source Initiative. Open source is also a viable business model: companies can charge for support or hosting around software that is itself free.
A shell reads what I type and passes it to the operating system. Commands can take arguments (what they act on) and options (flags that change behaviour), and the shell keeps a history I can scroll back through. Variables store values: local variables stay in the current session, environment variables pass to programs the shell starts, and PATH is the special variable listing where the shell looks for commands. Commands are either internal (built into the shell) or external (separate program files), and aliases and functions let me create my own shortcuts. Quoting controls how special characters are treated — double quotes still allow variable substitution, single quotes don't, a backslash escapes one character, and backquotes run a command and substitute its output. Control operators like ;, && and || chain commands together, running the next one unconditionally, only on success, or only on failure.
Linux has several built-in ways to find documentation. man <command> opens a command's manual page, organised into numbered sections, and can be searched by keyword. info gives more detailed, hyperlinked documentation for many GNU tools. which shows where a command's program file lives on disk, tying back to PATH, and --help gives a quick summary directly in the terminal when I just need a reminder.

Commands used 
man ls - opens the manual page ls, showing its decription , options and usage 
which ls- shows the full path to the ls progam file, confirming whee the shell finds it way
echo $PATH - prints teh lists of directories the shell searches, in order, when looking for a command
