history - shows the full history of the command used on the terminal 
!9 - reuse the command on number 9 of teh history 

How I Learned It
I attended the weekly discussion sessions, worked through the course modules on open source licensing, the shell, and finding documentation during personal study time, and practised the commands in my own Ubuntu terminal on WSL rather than only reading about them.

Key Takeaways
	•	Open source licensing gives users the right to see and change source code, unlike closed source software.
	•	The shell reads and interprets everything I type, including arguments, options, variables and quoting.
	•	The PATH variable determines which commands the shell can find and run by name.
	•	man, info and --help are three different depths of built-in documentation, and knowing all three means I'm never stuck on an unfamiliar command.
